{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you had a hundred euros to lend this \nweek, and you wanted a nice safe borrower,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bond market would suggest you lend it to \nL’Oréal… rather than the French Republic.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10720,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or to Air Liquide, a company whose main product \nis, broadly speaking, air. There is also LVMH,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or Sanofi, or (according to the credit \nstrategists at Barclays) a fairly wide  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "selection of triple-B rated companies - the \nbottom rung of what the rating agencies call  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investment grade. All of them can now \nborrow money more cheaply than France.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So can Italy… and so can Greece. \nWhich is a bit embarrassing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39520,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as French officials have spent the past fortnight \nreassuring everyone that France is not Greece.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46640,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bloomberg calculates that about 215 billion euros \nof French high-grade corporate bonds (or 38% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the market) now trade at lower yields than French \ngovernment debt of a similar maturity. At the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61200,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start of the year it was just 12 billion euros.\nNow, in theory, a government is the safest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "borrower in its own country. If things go wrong, \nit can always raise taxes or print more money.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "L’Oréal can’t do either of those things… it has \nno legal way of sending armed police to your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81360,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "house to collect seventy euros for moisturizer. \nFrance, as it happens, can’t print money either.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88240,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It handed that job over to the European Central \nBank in 1999, which we’ll come back to later. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it can still raise taxes, so investors have always \ngiven it the benefit of the doubt. Over the last  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few weeks, they’ve been rethinking that.\nFrench ten-year borrowing costs came within  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a whisker of 5% last week, the highest rate \nin almost a quarter of a century. The spread  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over German Bunds, which is how bond traders \nmeasure how nervous they are about a country,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120640,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "briefly topped one and a half percentage points, \nthe widest it has been since 2011. In eurozone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seating-plan terms, France has been moved from \nthe top table to the one near the bathroom. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While this has been going on, high school \nstudents across France have spent the past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few weeks blockading their schools in protest \nat teacher shortages, overcrowded classrooms,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145280,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and buildings that are falling apart. Most of the \nmarches have been peaceful - but some have not.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few entrance halls have been set on fire, and \nmore than six thousand people have been arrested,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most of them teenagers. Setting fire to your \nschool in protest at the condition of school  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buildings is a bold negotiating strategy… \nthough I suppose it does strengthen the case  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170320,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for renovation. The students would like more money \nspent on schools. The bond market would like less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money spent on everything. The French government \nis standing in between them, holding a budget.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185120,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "During the euro crisis, the bond market’s \ntroublemakers traveled as a group: the PIIGS,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which were Portugal, Ireland, Italy, Greece, and \nSpain. This time, as Lionel Laurent pointed out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Bloomberg this week, France has been given \nan acronym all of its own. FROGS – which stands  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for French Oversized Government and Social \nSecurity. It was coined by a Paris research  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210720,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "firm called Rexecode, so at least the insult was \nmade in France. I think it’s OK if they say it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves… I’m not sure if I’m allowed to say it. \nI’ll credit Rexecode every time, just to be safe.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As an Irishman, I take a strong interest \nin this sort of thing. I helped put the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231600,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“I” in PIIGS (well, my country did… I mostly just \nwatched the bond yields), so I’d like to formally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "welcome France to the club. The membership \nfee is about ten years of austerity, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the troika will soon be in touch.\nSo, before I dig into how France is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250960,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to handle its new membership, \nlet me tell you about this week’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254400,
    "dDurationMs": 90080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video sponsor, Incogni. [Sponsor clip]\nSo, to understand why the French bond market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344480,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is on fire, it helps to look at why everyone \nelse’s bond market is also smoking slightly. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For most of the last decade, borrowing money was \nclose to free, and governments (understandably)  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "took full advantage of the offer. Today, according \nto The Economist - the median ten-year government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365120,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bond in the rich world yields about 4%, the \nhighest in more than fifteen years. America’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372480,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ten-year yield topped 5.3% this week, and Japan’s \ncrossed 3% for the first time since 1996.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381520,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a few reasons for this. The first \nis AI. Goldman Sachs expects the five biggest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388480,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hyperscalers, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, \nand Oracle, to spend around $800 billion on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396000,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capital investment this year and $1.2 trillion \nnext year, which would be more than 3% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403120,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "America’s GDP. At first they paid for their data \ncenters out of their own cash flow. Now they’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410320,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "borrowing. They’ve issued $230 billion of bonds so \nfar this year, which is double last year’s total.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That money comes out of the same global pool \nof savings that governments borrow from,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the AI companies are not very price-sensitive \nborrowers. The FT reports that they see building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data centers as an existential necessity, \nand an extra percentage point or two of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437200,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest as a detail. The Economist says the \nhyperscalers now account for nearly 10% of new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "euro-denominated bonds sold by non-financial \ncompanies, and that some European firms  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451520,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "avoid issuing debt on the same days they do.\nThe second reason is the war in Iran. Crude oil  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is about 40% more expensive than it was before \nthe war started. This has kept inflation high  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pushed central banks into raising rates \nrather than cutting them. The Federal Reserve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470960,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "raised rates in September for the first time since \n2023, and the ECB has raised twice since June.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, when borrowing costs go up, every indebted \ngovernment gets tested. The arithmetic goes like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this. If your economy grows faster \nthan the interest rate on your debt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the debt shrinks as a share of national \nincome over time, as long as you aren’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "borrowing too much on top. If the interest rate \nis higher than your growth rate, the debt grows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501200,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative to the economy, unless you raise taxes \nor cut spending. Economists call this “r minus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "g,” which is about as exciting as it sounds.\nSome countries are getting through the test for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515120,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now, each for their own reasons. America runs a \ndeficit of about 6% of GDP, which The Economist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describes as unsustainable, but it issues the \nworld’s reserve currency, its economy is growing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529360,
    "dDurationMs": 10000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at over 3% a year, and investors still seem happy \nto lend to it. Japan’s net debt is 134% of GDP  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539360,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(its gross debt is a lot higher than that), but \ninflation has risen alongside interest rates,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so in real terms the debt is shrinking, \nand most of it is owed to the Japanese. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Britain’s thirty-year gilt yield is \nabove 6%, the highest since 1998,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557840,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but Britain already had its bond market revolt \nin 2022, when Liz Truss’s unfunded tax cuts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565360,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were outlasted by a lettuce. British politicians \nhave been nervous of the gilt market ever since.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572400,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then there’s France. It doesn’t issue a \nreserve currency, it isn’t having an AI boom,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578800,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it imports most of its oil and gas, and it hasn’t \nhad its Liz Truss moment… yet. As The Economist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "points out, French borrowing costs were about two \npercentage points above nominal GDP growth in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592640,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "second quarter, the widest gap in the rich world, \nand the gap has widened since. On those numbers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unless something changes, French debt keeps \ngrowing faster than the French economy. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, how hard is the arithmetic? Well, back \nin May, the French government asked four  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "independent economists to work out where the \ncountry’s public finances were heading. Their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617920,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "report came out in July, and it said that based on \ncurrent policies, the deficit would rise to 6.8%  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625600,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of GDP by 2030, that debt would pass 130% of \nGDP, and that the interest bill would grow by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634240,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about 10 billion euros a year. They also noted \nthat the interest bill was already approaching  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640800,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the budget of the entire Ministry of Education \n– and we’ll come back to that in a moment.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To stabilize the debt, the economists argued that \nFrance would need to make savings worth about 125  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654560,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billion euros a year by 2032. That calculation \nassumed France’s ten-year borrowing rate,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661520,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which was 3.7% at the time, would drift down to \n3.3% as the bond market rewarded the government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669680,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for behaving responsibly. That rate has recently \nrisen to about 4.9%. The report came out in July,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which in bond market terms makes it a \nhistorical document. Ben Hall at the FT  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684560,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reckons that the amount that needs to be saved per \nyear has already gone up to 140 billion euros.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692320,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Against that, Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu’s \n2027 budget proposes 43 billion euros of spending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700560,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cuts and tax rises. For one year, that is a big \nnumber. The problem is that something like it has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707920,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to happen again, and stick, every year until \n2032, and Lecornu doesn’t have a majority in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715520,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parliament. ING’s economists say that even if his \nplan passes in full, it won’t stabilize the debt.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Part of the problem, according to the \ncommission, is that the French state has come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728560,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see itself as the “insurer of last resort” \nfor every crisis. When something goes wrong,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the state steps in to protect household incomes \nand company balance sheets, and the emergency  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741280,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measures tend to stay in place long after the \nemergency has passed. The report calls this a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ratchet effect. In insurance terms, the French \nstate writes a policy for every disaster and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never lets one expire. This month, the queue of \nclaimants includes students, farmers, fishermen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "truckers, construction companies, and nurses.\nNormally, a government that spends too much can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768080,
    "dDurationMs": 10080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "raise taxes. But France’s tax take is already at \n44% of GDP, compared to an OECD average of 34%,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the commission says that it is the highest \nin the eurozone. The economists warn that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "public’s “consent to taxation” is weakening, \nbecause people feel they are paying more and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more for public services that are getting \nworse. People will put up with Scandinavian  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tax rates if they get Scandinavian schools. They \nget less enthusiastic when the roof is leaking.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802880,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which leaves the option of spending cuts. Almost \na quarter of public spending in France goes on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809520,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pensions. And French workers retire earlier, on \nmore generous terms, than in most rich countries.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While pensions aren’t legally untouchable, \nthey are politically radioactive. Lecornu’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "budget proposes freezing the most generous state \npensions, and the opposition parties are already  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828480,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lining up against it. Defense spending, meanwhile, \nis the one area that has been ring-fenced.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, back to the schools. The protests started \nin the middle of September at a lycée in Créteil,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just outside Paris, over long school days, \ncrumbling buildings, and teacher shortages.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848320,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Economist reports that spending per secondary \npupil has been flat in real terms for a decade.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two weeks into the protests, the government \npublished its budget, which cuts 1,588 teaching  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "posts, on the grounds that pupil numbers are \nfalling. As timing goes, that could have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better. One university student summed it \nup to The Economist: “The budget for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "army is increasing, but not for education.”\nOne student union reckons the students’ demands  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would cost about 10 billion euros. Lecornu \nhas promised measures that are “concrete,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "swift, hence, funded”… which he’ll \nannounce at the end of the month.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, the government is stuck. Every euro it tries \nto save brings someone new out onto the streets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and every year it doesn’t save anything, the \ninterest bill goes up by another ten billion.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which raises the difficult question… \nIf France is in this much trouble,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how is Italy, a country with more debt \nthan France, able to borrow more cheaply?\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To answer that, we need to go back to our \nofficial league table of borrowers that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bond market currently considers \nsafer than the French Republic. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Greece has been on it since the end of 2024. \nItaly joined last year. Emmanuel Moulin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the governor of the Banque de France, told \nthe FT this week that France is not Greece,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939440,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is true… they are entirely different \ncountries – but Greece does borrow more cheaply.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946320,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Italy is maybe more of a puzzle. The Economist \nputs Italy’s net debt at almost 130% of GDP,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954240,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while France’s total debt is just under 120%. \nNormally, if you owe more than someone else  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative to your income, you’d expect to be \nthe one paying the higher interest rate.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might think the bond market is \nrewarding Italy for some newfound  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "culture of enthusiastic tax compliance, but \nthat doesn’t seem to be the case either.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976640,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The FT reported this week that an estimated 60,000 \ncars and motorbikes, mostly in and around Naples,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are registered in Poland. Their Italian \nowners transfer their ownership to Polish  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989600,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shell companies, with the promise of saving about \n70% on road tax and insurance. Naples has some of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the highest car insurance premiums in Italy, \nand the road tax on a Fiat Panda is around 130  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003680,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to 140 euros a year. According to the Polish \ncar insurers’ agency, most of these vehicles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“have never been on the roads of Poland.”\nThere are other benefits too. Parking tickets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and speeding fines get sent to the shell company \nin Poland, where, as the same agency put it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“everything vanishes into thin air.” The Italian \nand Polish police are both aware of this. In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "March, police in Warsaw arrested seven people \nfor producing registration papers for more than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a thousand five hundred vehicles, with the help \nof what investigators described as corrupt local  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "officials. All of the cars had passed Polish \ntechnical inspections… which was impressive,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as none of them had ever been to Poland.\nWith an election coming in 2027, Giorgia  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meloni’s government has come up with a solution. \nIt’s suspending the road tax on small and midsized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060880,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cars for a year, which will leave about 2.4 \nbillion euros with Italian motorists rather  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the Italian treasury. This is possibly \nthe most effective anti-evasion policy ever  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073200,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "devised… it’s impossible to dodge a tax that \ndoesn’t exist. For now, the hole in the budget  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be filled with money left over from Italy’s \nEU-funded Covid recovery plan. Francesco Galietti  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087600,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the consultancy Policy Sonar described it \nto the FT as “giving some sugar to voters.” So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Italy is not exactly Switzerland. But it \nstill borrows more cheaply than France.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1101040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Different governments take different approaches \nto raising revenue. While France is searching  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106240,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down the back of every radiator in the country \nfor taxable euros, other countries are more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112800,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relaxed about it. In 2016, the European Commission \nordered Ireland (founding member of the PIIGS) to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1120480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collect 13 billion euros in back taxes from \nApple. The Irish government spent the next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eight years in the European courts, arguing that \nit didn’t want the money… which is a position  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1131920,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that would be hard to explain to a French finance \nminister. Ireland finally lost that case in 2024,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139440,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and roughly 14 billion euros, including interest, \nwas handed over. Having 14 billion euros deposited  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in your bank account against your will is \na heavy cross to bear, but as an Irishman,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1152960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I can tell you we accepted it with great dignity.\nSo, anyhow… back to the bond market.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If bond yields were a reward for paying your \nroad tax, Italy would not be beating France.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1166000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Growth and political stability matter too, \nof course. But the biggest difference between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1170720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the two countries comes down to one line \nin their accounts: the primary balance.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The primary balance is a government’s budget \nbefore interest. You take everything the state  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collects in taxes, subtract everything it \nspends on running the country, the schools,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1187680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pensions, the hospitals, and the army, \nand you ignore the interest on its debt.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1193040,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Italy runs a primary surplus. It collects more \nthan it spends on everything (if you exclude  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1199520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest), and it has done that in most years \nsince the early 1990s. It learned the hard way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1205920,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the 1992 currency crisis and again during the \neuro crisis, but it learned. Its overall deficit,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1214480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest included, is expected to \nfall below 3% of GDP this year.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1220400,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "France runs a primary deficit of 2.9% of \nGDP. So even if France had no debt at all,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1227680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and paid no interest to anyone, it would still \nbe borrowing to keep the lights on. It has run  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1233360,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a primary deficit every year since 2002, and it \nhasn’t balanced its overall budget since 1974.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1242000,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Italy has its own risks. It has an election \nin 2027, it has populists of its own,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1248800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and its deficit is expected to tick back \nup next year as it spends more on defense  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1254960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and on cushioning the energy shock. But the bond \nmarket has made a fairly practical calculation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1262000,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Italy has already had its crisis, and has shown \nthat it can run a surplus. France hasn’t… and,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1269040,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the past three weeks have shown, the political \ncost of even talking about an adjustment is high.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1277120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what does the French political class plan \nto do about all of this? The short answer is:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1283600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quite a lot, and not all of it adds up.\nLet’s start with the government. Emmanuel Macron  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1290400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "came to power in 2017 promising business-friendly \nreforms that would boost growth and fix the public  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1297360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finances. Over his nine years in office, the \nnational debt has grown by more than a trillion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1303120,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "euros, a lot of it spent protecting households \nand businesses from the pandemic and the energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1309200,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crisis. Since the 2024 snap election left France \nwith a hung parliament, budget negotiations have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1316400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cost two prime ministers their jobs. The current \none, Sébastien Lecornu, is trying to pass his 43  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1323760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billion euro package without a majority, and \nlawmakers are expected to rewrite much of it.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1330720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then there’s the opposition, which is preparing \nfor next April’s presidential election.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1336800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leading the polls is Marine Le Pen of the National \nRally, whose party has long championed generous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1343360,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state spending. On Tuesday, she announced that \nshe is now a champion of fiscal discipline.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1350000,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "She wants a constitutional “golden rule” capping \ndebt at 60% of GDP and cutting the deficit by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1358160,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "half a percentage point a year, backed by a \npackage of savings worth 140 billion euros by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1365600,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2032. Which is exactly the number Ben Hall \nat the FT came up with a few days earlier.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1373200,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The bond market liked it, and French ten-year \nyields fell by 0.12 percentage points that day.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1380320,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Economists were less convinced, as the plan \nalso includes cutting VAT on energy and food,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1387360,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and lowering the retirement age from 62 to 60 for \npeople who started work young. Olivier Blanchard,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1395120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the former chief economist of the IMF, told The \nEconomist: “Unfortunately, now that she has shown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1401680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where she intends to find the money, there is zero \ncredibility to her promise.” The finance minister,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1407760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Roland Lescure, said he had “the impression \nthat she has converted to fiscal discipline.”\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1414640,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the left, Jean-Luc Mélenchon, who is second in \nthe polls, has a more direct solution. According  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1421840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the FT, he has suggested that the French \nbonds held by the central bank could simply  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1427520,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be thrown “into the fire.” I suppose a bond \nfire would at least help with energy costs…\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1434160,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The formal version, from his party, is to convert \nabout 488 billion euros of French bonds held by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1441600,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Eurosystem into perpetual, zero-coupon \ndebt, which France would never repay and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1448240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never pay interest on. Daire MacFadden at the \nFT’s Unhedged newsletter, who used to work at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1454480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Bank of England and the European Central Bank, \npointed out the problem: the central bank created  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1460480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reserves to buy those bonds, and it still has \nto pay interest on those reserves to the banks.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1466560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Canceling the bonds just moves the cost to the \ncentral bank, while the bond market watches a G7  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1473600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "government default on its own central bank. \nFrance does have some history here. In 1789,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1480720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Louis the Sixteenth ran out of money, couldn’t \nget anyone to agree to new taxes, and called the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1486560,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Estates-General to sort it out. Things escalated \nfrom there. By 1797, with the king long since  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1494400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "executed, the Directory carried out what became \nknown as the “bankruptcy of the two-thirds,”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1500720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "writing off two-thirds of the national debt. \nThis solved the interest problem… The regime  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1507200,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that did it lasted two more years before Napoleon \ntook over. So, by French standards, the current  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1514240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "budget negotiations are going reasonably well.\nWith six months to go before the election,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1520080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bond market is looking at three main options: \na minority government that may not be able to pass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1525840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its budget, a frontrunner promising an earlier \nretirement age funded by spending discipline,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1532640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and her nearest rival proposing to settle \nthe national accounts with a bonfire. You  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1538400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can sort of understand why investors want to be \npaid more to lend to France than to Germany.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1544880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile, the frontrunner has already \nasked the European Central Bank to step  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1549920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in and “lighten the heavy interest burden,” \nas soon as France “takes back control” of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1555360,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its finances. Which brings us to Frankfurt.\nSo, why can’t the ECB just step in and fix this?\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1564080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, according to Morningstar, Jörg \nKrämer, the chief economist at Commerzbank,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1569680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sees four steps the ECB could take if things get \nworse. Step one is to sound less keen on raising  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1576720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest rates. Step two is verbal intervention, \nwhere central bankers solemnly declare that they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1583360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are ready to use their emergency tools. Step \nthree is to stop shrinking the ECB’s balance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1589520,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sheet and start reinvesting maturing bonds, with \nthe reinvestments tilted toward France. And step  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1596080,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "four is to press the big red button and activate \nthe Transmission Protection Instrument, or TPI.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1603920,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The TPI was created in 2022 to let the ECB buy \na country’s bonds, with no size limit set in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1612080,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advance, when market moves are “unwarranted” \nand “disorderly.” It has never been used. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1618800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slight catch is the eligibility criteria. To \nqualify, a country is supposed to be following  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1625120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sound and sustainable fiscal policies, and \nFrance is currently under the EU’s excessive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1630960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deficit procedure. According to Krämer, \nthat doesn’t automatically rule France out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1637120,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it doesn’t help. Now, just to be clear, \nthe ECB isn’t going to let France collapse.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1643920,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the TPI is an emergency rescue tool designed \nmainly for countries that don’t need rescuing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1651440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Using it on a government with a 5.4% \ndeficit that may not be able to pass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1656400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its budget would not go down well in Berlin.\nChristine Lagarde has been here before. In March  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1663760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2020, she told a press conference that the \nECB was “not here to close spreads.” Italian  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1670080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "borrowing costs jumped immediately, and she spent \nthe rest of the day walking the comment back.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1676560,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then there’s inflation. With the war in Iran \nkeeping energy prices high, the ECB has raised  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1683360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rates twice this year, and markets still see \na good chance of another hike by December.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1689840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every hike makes France’s debt more expensive. \nLagarde has started telling European lawmakers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1696560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that higher bond yields will slow the economy, \nwhich is a polite way of saying that the bond  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1702400,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market is now doing some of her job for her.\nAs The Economist notes, all of this sets up a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1709600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "game of chicken. Countries are supposed \nto fix their budgets before the ECB buys  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1714720,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their bonds. But the European Union can’t really \nsurvive without France, so if a populist French  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1722400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "president refused to cut spending and dared the \nECB to let French bonds collapse, The Economist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1729120,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reckons the president would probably win.\nGreece tried this in 2015. The ECB capped  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1736720,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emergency funding to Greek banks, the banks closed \nfor three weeks, and Greece gave in. But, as we’ve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1744000,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been told repeatedly, France is not Greece.\nSo, where does all of this leave France?\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1751440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lionel Laurent at Bloomberg described the \nsituation as less like Liz Truss’s sudden  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1757200,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blowup and more like “a slow squeeze.” And \nthat seems to me like the right diagnosis.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1764160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "France does have some real protections. French \nofficials point to high household savings,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1770400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reliable tax collection, and a stable banking \nsystem. But their biggest protection is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1777040,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shared currency - the euro, and this is \nwhere that decision from 1999 comes in.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1783680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As The Economist points out, French governments \nare still haunted by memories of May 1968, when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1790720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "students and workers shut the country down with \na general strike. The government settled it with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1796560,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a deal that raised the minimum wage by about a \nthird. Money quickly started leaving the country,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1803120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the Banque de France spent much of its \nreserves defending the franc. De Gaulle refused  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1808720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to devalue at the time, but a few months after \nhe left office in 1969, the franc was devalued by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1815760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about 11%. When a country has its own currency, \nthat’s where the pressure shows up first,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1822720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it tends to show up all at once.\nToday, the euro protects France from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1828800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a currency run, so the pressure arrives \nmore slowly: a spread over Germany that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1834160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keeps creeping wider, an interest bill that \neats a little more of the budget every year,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1839760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a steady turnover of prime ministers.\nFor now, the bond market is waiting.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1845920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Parliament will spend the next couple of months \ndeciding how much of Lecornu’s 43 billion euro  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1852080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "package it’s willing to keep. Moody’s is due to \nreview France’s credit rating later this month.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1858640,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And on October 29th, the ECB meets to \ndecide what to do about interest rates.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1865360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If nothing changes, the arithmetic the \neconomists laid out in July will keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1870640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "running in the background. The debt will keep \ngrowing, and the interest bill will keep rising  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1875920,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by about ten billion euros a year… and that’s \nusing July’s interest rates. Meanwhile, the bond  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1883360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market will keep updating its league table. \nIf you liked this video, you should watch my  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1888800,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video on Scott Bessent’s war on prices next. Don’t \nforget to check out this week’s sponsor, Incogni,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1896800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using the link in the description. \nSee you in the next video, bye."
    } ]
  } ]
}
